# width: 120
# height: 40
# `Ctrl+Q` with unsaved work raises a box that NAMES what is unsaved and
# offers [S]ave all / [Q]uit anyway / [C]ancel — the one confirm row every
# box in the app wears. Cancel has the focus, so Enter
# on a box you did not mean to raise is safe. Esc cancels, a second
# `Ctrl+Q` quits anyway, and a clean workspace never sees the box.

write one.txt "one\n"
write two.txt "two\n"
open one.txt
open two.txt

# Nothing raised yet. (The clean workspace's own box — Quit / Cancel —
# is `quit_confirm_clean.test`; this file is the unsaved-work one.)
expect screen lacks "Quit mnml?"

type CHANGED
expect dirty true
key ctrl+q
expect screen contains "Quit mnml?"
expect screen contains "Unsaved: two.txt"
expect screen contains "[S]ave all"
expect screen contains "[Q]uit anyway"
expect screen contains "[C]ancel"
# // changed (one-confirm): the buttons are bracketed and start from the
# box's left edge. The quit box used to paint padded labels right-aligned
# in a five-row box, the close box `[S]ave` from the left in a six-row
# one, and the two read as two different widgets.
# The whole row, borders included: a `contains` without them passes on a
# row-aligned box too (the buttons are only a cell or two narrower than
# the frame), so it would not catch a return of the right-aligned form.
expect screen contains "│   [S]ave all      [Q]uit anyway      [C]ancel     │"

# Esc cancels; the work is still there.
key esc
expect screen lacks "Quit mnml?"
expect dirty true
expect file two.txt lacks "CHANGED"

# Both buffers dirty: the box names both, in pane order.
command buffer.prev
expect pane "one.txt"
type CHANGED
key ctrl+q
expect screen contains "Unsaved: one.txt, two.txt"

# A click on Cancel answers it too. Row 15 is the button row — row 16 is
# the box's bottom border, and a click there dismisses the box like any
# click that misses, so it would have proved nothing. (Both moved down a
# row when the box grew from five rows to the one six-row shape; x 74 is
# still inside `  [C]ancel  `, which spans 69..80.)
click 74 15
expect screen lacks "Quit mnml?"
expect dirty true

# The hotkey letters fire their own button: `c` is Cancel.
key ctrl+q
expect screen contains "Quit mnml?"
key c
expect screen lacks "Quit mnml?"
expect dirty true

# Enter takes the focused Cancel, and says so — the box vanishing with
# nothing saved and nothing quit used to read as a key that did nothing
# (the delete box's cancel toast, the same rule).
key ctrl+q
expect screen contains "Quit mnml?"
key enter
expect screen lacks "Quit mnml?"
expect quit false
expect dirty true
expect screen contains "cancelled — nothing saved"

# The arrows move the focus off Cancel and Enter fires what they land on
# — left once from Cancel is Quit anyway, twice is Save all. Save all
# writes every dirty buffer and then ENDS THE SESSION (`cmd_app.zig`,
# "the quit box's Save all writes every dirty buffer and then quits"),
# so it is the last thing this file can do: the runner stops stepping an
# App that has quit, and only `expect quit` / `expect status` /
# `expect file` mean anything after one.
#
# Four lines used to follow the Enter below — a screen check and a
# `dirty` check — and they were read off a session that was already
# over, because the runner kept ticking and drawing a quit App. What is
# left is what survives the quit: the flag, and the files on disk.
key ctrl+q
key left
key left
expect quit false
key enter
expect quit true
expect file one.txt contains "CHANGED"
expect file two.txt contains "CHANGED"

# (`Quit anyway`, the `q` hotkey and a SECOND `Ctrl+Q` are the other
# three ways out of the box; each ends the session too, so each would
# need a file of its own. `cmd_app.zig` covers all three, and that the
# on-disk files are untouched.)
